\bars defines several bars at once. The bars of each
instrument are separated by |, and every instrument must
have the same number of bars.
Its syntax is the following:

\bars name {
    \time 4/4
    \inst1 c'4 d' e' f' | g'1 | e'2 c'
    \inst2 c2 g | c1 | g2 c
}

The name is given without the backslash. The bars are
stored as separate bars, named name-1, name-2, etc., and a
loop called name is created with all of them, so the line
below plays the three bars:

\name

\bars can also be written in one line:

\bars name { \inst1 c'1 | d'1 \inst2 c1 | g1 }

\time, \tempo, and \beatat can take one value for all the
bars, or one value per bar, separated by |:

    \time 4/4 | 3/4 | 4/4
    \tempo 4 = 60 | 4 = 90 | 4 = 120

With more than one value, there must be as many values as
bars.

An instrument line can include the name of a loop, which
is replaced by the bars of the loop, or copy an earlier bar
of the same set, also from another instrument:

    \inst2 c2 g | \name-1 | \name-1.\inst1

The same naming rules as with \bar apply.
